  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nThe original construction achieves C\u22480.380861, and the goal is to improve it to <0.3808. We'll use a multi-resolution optimization with adversarial perturbation strategy to overcome the limitations of the previous approach. This involves:\n1. Using an asymmetric parent initialization to avoid symmetry artifacts\n2. Employing gradient-based optimization with adaptive rejection procedures for infeasible candidates\n3. Incorporating a sparse representation with randomized spiking to address local optima\n4. Using a progressive resolution method: start at lower n_points to capture large-scale structure first, then gradually increase resolution with active-set optimization\n5. Introducing an anti-correlation adjustment module that explicitly targets the worst shifts identified through clustering\n\nKey innovations:\n- Resolution-refinement tree: progressively increase n_points (powers of 2) as optimization proceeds\n- Directed adversarial search: focuses on minimizing the worst overlap by considering lag statistics and transform domain representations\n- Hybrid optimization strategy: combining gradient-based local refinement with random search for directional exploration\n- Regularization scheme: balances flatness, sparsity, and self-similarity constraints to achieve sub-structure optimization\n\nMathematical specification\nWe optimize a step function h:[0,2]\u2192[0,1] with \u222bh dx = 1 to minimize:\nC\u2085 = max\u2096 \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx\n\nRepresentation:\n- Discretized over grid points at varying n (default 256)\n- Value representation as double-precision floats\n- Sparse initialization to penalize entropy negatively\n\nObjective: minimize C\u2085\nConstraints:\n1. h(x) \u2208 [0,1] \u2200x\n2. \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1\n\nCorrelation computation:\n- Full correlation mode (same as in evaluator)\n- dx = 2/n\n- Normalization by dx implicitly handled\n\nSearch space:\n- n_points chosen from {128, 256, 512, 1024, 2048}\n- h_i \u2208 [0,1], log-constrained sum log(h_i + \u03b5) = n/2 (modified to account for clipping issues)\n\nImplementation procedure\n1. Runtime initialization:\n   - Read initial_h_values if available (n=500)\n   - Convert to sparse representation for faster processing\n   - Initialize random seed from provided seed\n\n2. Multi-stage procedure:\n   a) Coarse-scale exploration with resolution 128 points\n   b) Progressive resolution doubling up to 2048\n   c) Local search with projected gradients at each resolution level\n   d) Cross-resolution parameter inheritance with progressive constraint tightening\n   e) Final solution validation with full 1D numerical integration\n\n3. Core candidate generation:\n   At resolution n:\n   - Start from previous level solution with grid refinement\n   - Apply multiple perturbation schemes:\n     \u25b6  Random injection (random points \u00b10.3)\n     \u25b6  Wave-like variations (sine waves at multiple frequencies)\n     \u25b6  Spike-smoothing (combining sparse/continuous structures)\n   - Adjust for scale-dependent worst shifts via transform-domain modification\n   - Project to [0,1] and satisfy integral constraint\n\n4. Feasibility and termination checks:\n   - Check sum(h) = n/2 with tolerance 1e-5\n   - Reject candidates that violate h_i \u2208 [0,1] on any point\n   - Monitor computation budget (1100s total)\n   - Check for redundancy in optimization progress\n\nSearch choices and variations\nBase configuration:\n- n_start = 128 (with parent inheritance)\n- n_levels = [128, 256, 512, 1024, 2048]\n- Optimization per level: 15-30 iterations of gradient descent (L-BFGS-B with analytic gradient)\n- Active set selection: top 8 most negative shifted correlations, weighted by Z-score\n- Huberization parameter for gradient: 0.003\n- Cooling schedule: progressively reduce allowed moves as resolution increases\n\nKey variations:\n- A: Hybrid exploration: 70% gradient-based, 30% random patch insertion\n- B: Wave optimization: oscillatory pattern embedding for phase symmetry breaking\n- C: Adaptive constraint: dynamic penalty based on current min shift\n- D: Batched least-squares: periodic full-space least-squares at final level\n- E: Transform-domain: singular values clipping in discrete cosine transform\n\nAll variations are parallelized with early termination if worse than current best.\n\nComputebudget and robustness\nEstimation:\n- Level 1 (n=128): ~5 iterations, 1 minute\n- Level 2-3: ~10 per level, 8-9 minutes\n- Level 4-5: ~15 per level, 12-14 minutes\n- Budget safeguards at each level to prevent overflow\n\nUpper-level control:\n- For level i, check: time_remaining/iterations > timereduction factor\n- Early termination if improvement stalls (including convergence criteria)\n- Valid fallback: if stuck, revert to parent solution at previous level\n\nValidation and return contract\n1. Final candidate must satisfy:\n   - len(h_values) = target_n (= final resolution)\n   - 0 \u2264 h_values[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n   - \u2211 h_values \u2248 n/2 (coarse projection)\n   - C\u2085 = adapt_from_parent(?) (exact evaluator consistency)\n\n2. Re-evaluation protocol:\n   - Run compute_c5 directly on all returned candidates (all n_levels?)\n   - For each candidate, compute h.flat.correlate(1-h) in debug mode\n   - Return the candidate with lowest C\u2085\n\n3. Format compliance:\n   - Output tuple: (h_values, c5_bound, n_points)\n   - n_points must match the resolution level\n\nFallback strategy:\n1. On initial timeout, use parent solution at previous level\n2. On detection of NaN/Inf, revert to coarse solution\n3. If solver fails with infeasible solution, project and retry\n\nReturn specification:\n- h_values: NumPy array of length n_points\n- c5_bound: scalar value (exact according to evaluator)\n- n_points: integer (coarse to fine refinement)\n\nCandidate selection criteria:\n- Primary: minimize final C\u2085\n- Secondary: maximize feasibility/sparse representation\n- Timereduction: only run full evaluations if > budget/3 remain\n\nWatchdog:\n- Terminate process if (run time > 1000s) to leave timely output\n- Periodic health checks at factor-2 timereduction points\n- Memory monitoring: reduce resolution if swapping occurs\n\nVisual validation (if time permits):\n- Plot active shift distributions and heatmap of samples\n- Verify gradient approximations against finite differences\n\nNotes:\n1. The parent solution inherits from initial provided values\n2. All operations use analytic gradient computation where possible\n3. Conversions between resolutions are done via adaptive downsampling/reconstruction\n4. Transformed methods use scipy.fft correlate with square padding for multi-resolution support",
